== Chapter I

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[Indwelling sin in believers, treated by the apostle in Rom 7:21. The place explained.]
]

IT is #emph[indwelling sin] that we intend to treat, and that in its remainders in persons after their conversion to God, with its power, efficacy, and effects. This is also the great aim of the apostle in #emph[chapter] 7 of the Epistle to the #emph[Romans]: to show and prove it. There are indeed many disputes about the apostle’s main purpose in that #emph[chapter], and about the state of the person whose condition he describes there, whether under the law or under grace. I will not enter into that dispute at present. I will take for granted what may be undeniably proved and shown, namely, that what is set out there is the condition of a #emph[regenerate person] with respect to the remaining power of indwelling sin, and that it is illustrated by, and in, the person of the apostle himself. In that discourse of his, therefore, the foundation will be laid for what we have to offer on this subject. Not that I will go through an exposition of his revelation of this truth as it lies in its own context; I will only make use of what he delivers, as occasion offers. And here the first thing we meet is what he affirms in verse 21: “So I find this law: when I want to do good, evil is present with me.”

There are #emph[four things] to be observed in these words.

First, the name he gives to #emph[indwelling sin], by which he expresses its power and efficacy: it is a #emph[law]. For what he calls a #emph[law] in this verse he calls in the verse before “sin that dwells in him.”

Secondly, the way by which he came to discover this #emph[law]. He discovered it not absolutely and in its own nature, but in himself: he #emph[found] it. “I find this law.”

Thirdly, the frame of his soul and inner man along with this law of sin, and under the discovery of it: “he wants to do good.”

Fourthly, the state and activity of this #emph[law] when the soul is in that frame, when it wants to do good: “it is present with him.” For what ends and purposes we will show later.

The first thing to be observed is the name used here by the apostle: he calls #emph[indwelling sin] a #emph[law]. It is a #emph[law].

A #emph[law] is taken either properly, for a #emph[directive rule], or improperly, for an #emph[operative, effective principle] which seems to have the force of a law. In its first sense it is a moral rule, which directs and commands, and in various ways moves and governs the mind and the will as to the things it requires or forbids. This is plainly the general nature and work of a law. It commands some things and forbids others, with #emph[rewards] and penalties, which move and drive men to do the one and avoid the other. From this, in a secondary sense, an #emph[inward principle] that moves and inclines constantly to any actions is called a law. The principle that is in the #emph[nature] of every thing, moving and carrying it toward its own end and rest, is called the #emph[law of nature]. In this respect every inward principle that inclines and urges to workings or actions suited to itself is a law. So in Rom 8:2 the powerful and effectual working of the Spirit and grace of Christ in the hearts of believers is called the #emph[law of the Spirit of life]. And for this reason the apostle here calls #emph[indwelling sin] a law. It is a powerful and effectual indwelling principle, inclining and pressing to actions agreeable and suited to its own nature. This, and nothing else, is the apostle’s meaning in this expression. The term “a law” may sometimes mean a #emph[state] and #emph[condition]. If it were used so here, the meaning of the words would be: “I find that this is my condition, this is how things stand with me,” that when I want to do good, evil is present with me. That makes no great change in the main meaning of the place. Yet properly it can mean nothing here but the chief #emph[subject] being treated. For although the apostle uses the name of a law in various ways in this chapter, yet when it relates to #emph[sin] he nowhere applies it to the condition of the person, but only to express either the nature or the power of sin itself. So in verse 23: “But I see another law at work in my members, warring against the law of my mind and holding me captive to the law of sin that dwells within me.” What he here calls the #emph[law of his mind], from its chief subject and seat, is in itself nothing other than the #emph[law of the Spirit of life] which is in Christ Jesus (Rom 8:2), or the effectual power of the Spirit of grace, as was said. But the #emph[law], as applied to #emph[sin], has a double sense. In the first place, “I see a law in my members,” it means the being and nature of sin. In the latter, “holding me captive to the law of sin that dwells within me,” it means its power and efficacy. And both of these are included in the same name used alone in verse 20. Now what we observe from this #emph[name] or term of a #emph[law] given to sin is this: #emph[There is an exceeding efficacy and power in the remainders of indwelling sin in believers, with a constant working toward evil.]

So it is in believers. It is a law even #emph[in them], though not #emph[to them]. Though its rule is broken, its strength weakened and impaired, and its root mortified, yet it is still a #emph[law] of great force and efficacy. Where it is least felt, it is most powerful. Carnal men, with respect to spiritual and moral duties, are nothing but this law; they do nothing except from it and by it. In them it is a ruling and prevailing principle of all #emph[moral] actions with respect to a supernatural and eternal end. I will not consider it in those in whom it has most power, but in those in whom its power is chiefly discovered and discerned, that is, in believers; in the others only so far as it serves the further conviction and display of it.

Secondly, the apostle sets out the way by which he discovered this law in himself: εὑρίσκω ἄρα τὸν νόμον, “I find then,” or therefore, a #emph[law]. He #emph[found] it. He had been #emph[told] that there was such a law; it had been #emph[preached] to him. This convinced him that there was a #emph[law of sin]. But it is one thing for a man to know in general that there is a #emph[law of sin], and another for a man to have #emph[experience] of the power of this law of sin in himself. It is preached to all, and all men who acknowledge the Scripture admit it, since it is declared there. But only a few #emph[know] it in themselves; otherwise we would have more complaints about it than we have, more striving against it, and less of its fruits in the world. But this is what the apostle affirms: not that the #emph[doctrine] of it had been preached to him, but that he found it by experience in himself. “I find this law”: I have experience of its power and efficacy. For a man to find his sickness, and his danger from it, by its effects is a different thing from hearing a discourse about a disease from its causes. And this experience is the great preserver of all divine truths in the soul. This is to know a thing indeed, in reality: #emph[to know it for ourselves], when we find in ourselves what we are taught from the word. From this we observe,

Secondly, #emph[believers have experience of the power and efficacy of indwelling sin.] They #emph[find] it in themselves; they #emph[find] it as a law. It has an efficacy that makes itself evident to those who are #emph[alive] to discern it. Those who do not find its power are under its dominion. Whoever strives against it will know and find that it is present with him and that it is powerful in him. He who swims against the #emph[stream] will find it strong, though he who drifts along with it does not feel it.

Thirdly, the general frame of believers, in spite of the dwelling of #emph[this law of sin] in them, is also expressed here: “they want to do good.” This law is present θέλοντι ἐμοὶ ποιεῖν τὸ καλόν [with me, who want to do good]. The habitual inclination of their will is toward good. This law in them is not a law to them, as it is to unbelievers. They are not #emph[wholly] subject to its power, nor #emph[morally] bound to its commands. Grace has the sovereignty in their souls, and grace gives them a #emph[will] to good. “They want to do good,” that is, always and constantly. In 1 John 3:9, ποιεῖν ἁμαρτίαν, “to commit sin,” is to make a trade of sin, to make sinning a man’s business; and in that sense it is said that a believer does not “commit sin.” And so ποιεῖν τὸ καλόν, to do what is good, to will to do so, is to have the #emph[habitual] bent and inclination of the will set on what is good, that is, morally and spiritually good, which is the proper subject being treated. From this comes our third observation:

There is in believers, and it is kept up in them through grace, a constant and ordinarily prevailing will to do good, in spite of the power and efficacy of indwelling sin to the contrary.

This distinguishes believers in their #emph[worst] condition from unbelievers in their #emph[best]. The #emph[will] in unbelievers is under the power of the law of sin. The opposition they make to sin, whether in its root or its branches, comes from their light and their consciences; the #emph[will to sin] is never taken away in them. Take away all other considerations and hindrances (which we will treat later), and they would always sin willingly. Their faint efforts to answer their convictions are far from #emph[a will to do what is good]. They will indeed plead that they would leave their sins if they could, and that they would gladly do better than they do. But what they mean by that expression is the #emph[working] of their light and convictions, not any spiritual #emph[inclination] of their wills. For where there is a will to do good, there is a choice of what is good for the sake of its own excellence, because it is desirable and suited to the soul, and therefore to be preferred before what is contrary to it. Now this is not in any unbelievers. They do not, and cannot, choose what is spiritually good in this way, nor is it so excellent or suited to any principle that is in them. They only have some desires to attain the #emph[end] to which what is good leads, and to avoid the evil that the neglect of it tends to. And in many of them even these desires are, for the most part, so weak and feeble that they do not put them on any considerable effort. Witness the luxury, sloth, worldliness and carnal security in which the generality of men are drowned. But in believers there is a will to do good, a habitual disposition and inclination in their wills toward what is spiritually good. And where this is, it is accompanied with corresponding effects. The will is the principle of our moral actions, and so the general course of our actions will be suited to its #emph[prevailing disposition]. Good things will come from the good treasure of the heart. Nor can this disposition be shown to be in anyone except by its fruits. A will to do good without doing good is only a pretense.

Fourthly, there is yet another thing remaining in these words of the apostle. It arises from the relation that the #emph[presence] of sin has to the #emph[time] and #emph[season] of #emph[duty]: “When I want to do good” (he says), “evil is present with me.”

There are two things to be considered in the #emph[will to do good] that is in believers.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, there is its #emph[habitual residence] in them. They always have a #emph[habitual inclination of will] toward what is good. And this habitual readiness for good is always present with them, as the apostle expresses it in verse 18 of this chapter.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, there are special #emph[times] and #emph[seasons] for the exercise of that principle. There is a “when I want to do good”: a season in which this or that good, this or that duty, is to be performed and accomplished, in keeping with the habitual readiness and inclination of the will.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[To these two, two things in #emph[indwelling sin] are opposed. To the #emph[gracious principle] residing in the will, inclining to what is spiritually good, it is opposed as a #emph[law], that is, a #emph[contrary principle] inclining to evil, with an aversion from what is good. To the second, the actual willing of this or that particular good, to this “when I want to do good,” is opposed the presence of this law: “evil is present with me,” παράκειταί μοι τὸ κακόν. Evil is at hand and ready to oppose the #emph[actual accomplishment] of the good aimed at. From this,]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Fourthly,] indwelling sin is effectually at work, rebelling and inclining to evil, when the will to do good is particularly active and inclining to obedience.]

And this is the description of a man who is a believer and a sinner; for everyone who is the #emph[former] is the #emph[latter] also. These are the contrary principles and the contrary workings that are in him. The principles are a #emph[will to do good] on the one hand, from grace, and a #emph[law of sin] on the other. Their opposing actions and workings are hinted at in the words, “When I want to do good, evil is present with me.” And both are more fully expressed by the apostle in Gal 5:17: “For the flesh lusts against the Spirit, and the Spirit against the flesh. They are opposed to each other, so that I cannot do what I want.”

And here lie the springs of the whole course of our obedience. An acquaintance with these different principles and their workings is the chief part of our wisdom. In substance, next to the free grace of God in our justification by the blood of Christ, they are the only things in which the glory of God and our own souls are concerned. These are the springs of our holiness and of our sins, of our joys and troubles, of our refreshments and sorrows. It concerns all of us, then, who intend to walk with God or to glorify him in this world, to be thoroughly acquainted with these things.

And from this we may see what #emph[wisdom] is required in guiding and managing our hearts and ways before God. Where the #emph[subjects] of a ruler are in feuds and conflict with one another, unless great #emph[wisdom] is used in governing the whole, everything in that state will quickly go to ruin. These contrary principles are in the hearts of believers, and if they do not labor to be spiritually wise, how will they be able to steer their course rightly? Many men live in the dark to themselves all their days; whatever else they know, they do not know themselves. They know their outward estates, how #emph[rich] they are, and they are careful to examine the condition of their bodies as to health and sickness. But as to their #emph[inner man], and their principles with respect to God and eternity, they know little or nothing of themselves. Indeed, few labor to grow #emph[wise] in this matter. Few study themselves as they ought, or are acquainted with the evil of their own hearts as they ought; and yet on this depends the whole course of their obedience, and so their eternal condition. This therefore is our #emph[wisdom], and it is a needful wisdom, if we have any intention to please God, or to avoid what is a provocation to the eyes of his glory.

We will also find in our inquiry into this what #emph[diligence] and watchfulness is required for a Christian life. There is a constant #emph[enemy] to it in everyone’s heart. What an enemy it is we will show later, for this is our aim: to expose him to the utmost. In the meantime we may well lament the woeful sloth and negligence that is in most people, even in professors. They live and walk as though they intended to go to heaven blindfolded and asleep, as though they had no #emph[enemy] to deal with. So their mistake and folly will be fully laid open as we go on.

What I will chiefly fix on from this place of the apostle, with respect to our present purpose, is what was laid down first, namely, that #emph[there is an exceeding efficacy and power in the remainder of indwelling sin in believers, with a constant inclination and working toward evil.]

#emph[Awake], therefore, all of you#super[+] in whose hearts there is anything of the ways of God. Your#super[+] #emph[enemy] is not only #emph[upon] you#super[+], as on #emph[Samson] of old, but is #emph[in you#super[+]] too. He is at work by every means of #emph[force] and #emph[cunning], as we will see. Would you#super[+] not dishonor God and his gospel? Would you#super[+] not give offense to the saints and the ways of God? Would you#super[+] not wound your#super[+] consciences and endanger your#super[+] souls? Would you#super[+] not grieve the good and Holy Spirit of God, the author of all your#super[+] comforts? Would you#super[+] keep your#super[+] garments undefiled, and escape the woeful temptations and pollutions of the days in which we live? Would you#super[+] be kept from the number of the #emph[apostates] in these latter days? Then awake to the consideration of this cursed #emph[enemy]. It is the spring of all these and countless other evils, and also of the ruin of all the souls that perish in this world.
